<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Protest and freedom · Anthem · 1929 · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Bayan Ko</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Filipino song of love for the homeland, first written in Spanish and then in Tagalog in 1929, sung for freedom in protests.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>José Alejandrino (Spanish original, &quot;Nuestra patria&quot;); José Corazón de Jesús (Tagalog version, 1929)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Constancio de Guzmán</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Filipinos; Philippine protest movements</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tagalog, Spanish</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Philippines</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>Spanish lyrics written during the American occupation, for the zarzuela Walang Sugat; Tagalog version 1929</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Protest and freedom · Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (Tagalog words by José Corazón de Jesús (1929; he died in 1932); Spanish words by José Alejandrino; music by Constancio de Guzmán)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayan-ko">Bayan Ko</a>&quot;, usually translated as &quot;My Country&quot;, is a Filipino patriotic song in the form of a kundiman. Its words were first written in Spanish, as &quot;Nuestra patria&quot; (&quot;Our Motherland&quot;), for Severino Reyes's zarzuela Walang Sugat (&quot;No Wound&quot;). They are attributed to the revolutionary general José Alejandrino, a propagandista, and were written in light of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-american-war">Philippine–American War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/occupation-of-japan">American occupation</a> that followed, which the song opposed. Some three decades later the poet José Corazón de Jesús translated them into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagalog-language">Tagalog</a>; this 1929 Tagalog version, with music attributed to Constancio de Guzmán, is the one in general use.</p><p>The song is often considered the unofficial second national anthem of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, and groups of overseas Filipinos sometimes sing it after &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lupang-hinirang">Lupang Hinirang</a>&quot;, the national anthem, or on its own. Because it is so widely known it is sometimes taken for a folk song, and because of what its words say it has been used as a protest song by different political groups at various points in Philippine history.</p><p>The song regained wide popularity during the Marcos <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>, when protesters sang their own version of it. After President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-marcos">Ferdinand Marcos</a> imposed martial law in 1972, the song was deemed seditious and its public performance was banned, with violators facing arrest and detention. In the 1970s activists of the National Democratic movement sang a version with altered words and an added closing verse of liberation. Crowds sang it at the funeral of Senator Benigno Aquino Jr. in 1983 and during the People Power Revolution of 1986, when the singer Freddie Aguilar led them; Aguilar's recording became one of its best-known renditions.</p><p>Since the 1986 revolution, which overthrew the Marcos government and opened the Fifth Republic, the song has been associated with anti-government protest. In February 1987 a cover by the pop singer JoAnne Lorenzana was broadcast on radio and television for the revolution's first anniversary, as part of a nationalistic campaign by PLDT. In 1984 Lino Brocka's film <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayan-ko">Bayan Ko</a>: Kapit sa Patalim took its title from the song, but the song itself was censored from the film as a condition of its release in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>.</p><p>The song has also marked public mourning. After the death of former President Corazón Aquino on 1 August 2009 it was sung at the EDSA Shrine, by crowds as her remains were taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> Cathedral on 3 August, by Lea Salonga at the Requiem Mass on 5 August, and by mourners at her entombment in Parañaque. The University of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> Madrigal Singers sang it at the inauguration of President Benigno Aquino III and Vice-President Jejomar Binay on 30 June 2010, and mourners sang it again when his ashes were buried at the Manila Memorial Park after his death on 24 June 2021. It was sung in nationwide protests after the burial of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-marcos">Ferdinand Marcos</a> at the Libingan ng mga Bayani in 2016, and in protests against President Rodrigo Duterte in 2020.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A love song addressed to the homeland, in the manner of the kundiman. It praises the country's beauty, grieves that it is held in bondage, and ends on the singer's longing to see it wholly free. The mood moves from tenderness to sorrow and hope, which is why protesters could turn it against any government they saw as oppressive.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Tagalog</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Tagalog</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tl">Ang bayan kong Pilipinas,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>My country, the Philippines,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tl">lupain ng ginto’t bulaklak.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>land of gold and flowers.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tl">Pag-ibig ang sa kaniyáng palad,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Love, which was in her palm,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tl">nag-alay ng ganda’t dilág.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>offered beauty and splendour.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tl">At sa kaniyáng yumi at ganda,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And by her grace and beauty</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tl">dayuhan ay nahalina.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the foreigner was enticed.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tl">Bayan ko, binihag ka,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>My country, you were taken captive,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tl">nasadlak sa dusa.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>cast down into suffering.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tl">Ibon mang may layang lumipad,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Even a bird that is free to fly,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tl">kulungin mo at umiiyak!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>cage it and it weeps!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tl">Bayan pa kayáng sakdal-dilag,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>How much more a country, so splendid,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tl">ang ‘di magnasang makaalpas?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that would not long to break free?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tl">Pilipinas kong minumutya,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>My Philippines, whom I treasure,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tl">pugad ng luhá ko’t dalita,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>nest of my tears and misery,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tl">aking adhika:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>my aspiration:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tl">makita kang sakdal laya!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to see you wholly free!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: José Corazón de Jesús (Tagalog words, 1929). Text from <a href="https://tl.wikisource.org/wiki/Bayan_Ko" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (6)</summary><ul><li>line 1.3: 'palad' is the palm of the hand and also one's lot or fate; 'Pag-ibig ang sa kaniyang palad' can read 'Love was in her palm' or 'Love was her lot'. The sense of 1.3-4: love, which she held (or which was her destiny), gave her beauty and splendour.</li><li>line 1.4 and 2.3: 'dilag' is radiance, splendour; 'sakdal-dilag' (2.3) is 'perfectly splendid'.</li><li>line 1.5: 'yumi' is modesty, gentle grace.</li><li>line 2.3-4: one rhetorical question: if even a bird weeps when caged, how much more a country; 'Bayan pa kaya' is 'how much more, then, a country'.</li><li>line 2.5: 'minumutya' is from 'mutya', a pearl or treasure: 'whom I cherish as a pearl'.</li><li>Source metadata: the import puts José Corazón de Jesús in the 'translator' field; he wrote these Tagalog words (1929) to Constancio de Guzmán's music, so he is the author of the text, not a translator.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="pWLEg9HRLds" data-title="Bayan Ko: Freddie Aguilar's recording"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Freddie Aguilar's recording</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Freddie Aguilar - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pWLEg9HRLds" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Freddie Aguilar's rendition (instrumental section from &quot;Pilipinas Kong Mahal&quot;)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement, sung at the 1986 revolution</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">National Democratic activists' version</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1970s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">altered words and an added coda</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Bayan Ko: Kapit sa Patalim (film, Lino Brocka)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1984</span><span class="sg-chain-h">title taken from the song (the song itself censored from the film)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">JoAnne Lorenzana's cover for PLDT's campaign</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1987</span><span class="sg-chain-h">cover</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Asin's rendition (with José Corazón de Jesús's &quot;Kay Sarap Mabuhay sa Sariling Bayan&quot; as prelude)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement with altered words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Towering Power: A Musical Dedication (Josephine Roberto and the cast of That's Entertainment)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1988</span><span class="sg-chain-h">a cappella version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Allison Opaon's Japanese version, Yokohama</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2006</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Libera's arrangement by Robert Prizeman</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2009</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Lucio D. San Pedro's arrangement</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Bayan Ko (television series)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2013</span><span class="sg-chain-h">title taken from the song</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Walang Sugat (zarzuela by Severino Reyes)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the Spanish lyrics were written for it</span></li></ul></section>
